---
import Page from '../../layouts/Page.astro';

const statements = [
	'In most ways my life is close to my ideal.',
	'The conditions of my life are excellent.',
	'I am satisfied with my life.',
	'So far I have gotten the important things I want in life.',
	'If I could live my life over, I would change almost nothing.',
];

const ratings = [
	{ value: 7, label: 'Strongly agree' },
	{ value: 6, label: 'Agree' },
	{ value: 5, label: 'Slightly agree' },
	{ value: 4, label: 'Neither agree nor disagree' },
	{ value: 3, label: 'Slightly disagree' },
	{ value: 2, label: 'Disagree' },
	{ value: 1, label: 'Strongly disagree' },
];
---

<Page
	title="Satisfaction with Life Scale (SWLS): free online test with scoring"
	description="Complete the Satisfaction with Life Scale online and get your score out of 35 with Diener's interpretation bands. Five questions, under a minute. Free, no sign-up required."
>
	<Fragment slot="head">
			<script type="application/ld+json">
	{
	  "@context": "https://schema.org",
	  "@graph": [
	    {
	      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
	      "itemListElement": [
	        {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": "https://www.postviralrecovery.com/"},
	        {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Tools", "item": "https://www.postviralrecovery.com/tools/"},
	        {"@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Satisfaction with Life Scale", "item": "https://www.postviralrecovery.com/tools/satisfaction-with-life-scale/"}
	      ]
	    },
	    {
	      "@type": "FAQPage",
	      "mainEntity": [
	        {
	          "@type": "Question",
	          "name": "What is the Satisfaction with Life Scale?",
	          "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "The SWLS is a five-item measure of how satisfied a person is with their life as a whole, published by Ed Diener and colleagues in 1985. You rate five statements from strongly disagree to strongly agree, giving a total between 5 and 35. It is one of the most widely used well-being measures in psychology and is free to use."}
	        },
	        {
	          "@type": "Question",
	          "name": "What is a normal Satisfaction with Life Scale score?",
	          "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "Diener's bands are: 31 to 35 extremely satisfied, 26 to 30 satisfied, 21 to 25 slightly satisfied, 20 neutral, 15 to 19 slightly dissatisfied, 10 to 14 dissatisfied, and 5 to 9 extremely dissatisfied. Most people in Western general-population samples score between 21 and 30."}
	        },
	        {
	          "@type": "Question",
	          "name": "How is the SWLS different from the WHO-5?",
	          "acceptedAnswer": {"@type": "Answer", "text": "The WHO-5 asks how you have felt over the past two weeks, so it moves with mood, sleep and symptoms. The SWLS asks for a considered judgement of your life as a whole, with no time frame, so it is more stable and reflects how well your life matches what you want from it. In a long illness the two can diverge: a poor fortnight need not change how you judge your life, and the reverse."}
	        }
	      ]
	    }
	  ]
	}
			</script>
	</Fragment>
			<div class="tool-header">
				<a href="/tools" class="section-label tools">Tools</a>
				<h1>Satisfaction with Life Scale (SWLS)</h1>
				<p class="subtitle">Five statements about your life as a whole, scored 5–35</p>
				<p class="description">
					The Satisfaction with Life Scale was developed by Ed Diener and colleagues in 1985 to measure the cognitive side of well-being: not how you feel today, but your own judgement of how your life is going, measured against your own standards rather than anyone else's. It has five statements, takes under a minute, and has been used in thousands of studies across ages, cultures and health conditions. Diener released it into the public domain.
				</p>
				<p class="description">
					For someone living with a post-viral condition it captures something the symptom scales do not. Illness can take a great deal from a life, and people vary enormously in how far it dents their overall satisfaction. Re-taking the SWLS every few months, next to the <a href="/tools/who-5/">WHO-5</a> for recent mood, gives a sense of whether adaptation, treatment or changes in circumstance are moving the bigger picture.
				</p>
				<div class="disclaimer">
					<strong>This is not a diagnostic tool.</strong> A low score means you are not satisfied with your life at the moment, which in a long illness is an understandable position, not a disorder. If you are struggling, tell your GP. Source: Diener E, Emmons RA, Larsen RJ, Griffin S. <em>The Satisfaction with Life Scale.</em> J Pers Assess. 1985;49(1):71–75. Interpretation bands from Pavot W, Diener E. <em>Review of the Satisfaction with Life Scale.</em> Psychol Assess. 1993;5(2):164–172. The scale is in the public domain and reproduced here with attribution.
				</div>
			</div>

			<form id="swls-form" novalidate>
				<p class="form-instructions">Below are five statements that you may agree or disagree with. Be open and honest in your responding. There is no time frame: answer for your life as it is now.</p>

				<div class="question-grid">
					{statements.map((q, i) => (
						<div class="question" data-index={i}>
							<p class="q-text"><span class="q-num">{i + 1}.</span> <strong>{q}</strong></p>
							<div class="options">
								{ratings.map(r => (
									<label>
										<input type="radio" name={`q${i}`} value={r.value} required />
										<span>{r.label}</span>
									</label>
								))}
							</div>
						</div>
					))}
				</div>

				<div class="form-footer">
					<button type="submit">Calculate score</button>
					<button type="button" id="reset-btn">Clear all</button>
				</div>
			</form>

			<div id="results" class="results" hidden>
				<h2>Your SWLS score</h2>
				<div class="total-score">
					<span id="total-score-value">—</span>
					<span class="total-score-label">out of 35</span>
				</div>
				<p id="score-interpretation" class="score-interp"></p>

				<div class="results-disclaimer">
					<h3>Understanding your score</h3>
					<p>The five answers are added together. Diener's interpretation bands are: <strong>31–35</strong> extremely satisfied, <strong>26–30</strong> satisfied, <strong>21–25</strong> slightly satisfied, <strong>20</strong> neutral, <strong>15–19</strong> slightly dissatisfied, <strong>10–14</strong> dissatisfied, <strong>5–9</strong> extremely dissatisfied. Most people in Western general-population samples score between 21 and 30.</p>
					<p>The scale is deliberately global. It does not ask about health, work or relationships separately, because people weight those areas differently. That also means a score does not tell you <em>why</em> you feel as you do; it tells you where you have landed. If you want to know what is pulling the number down, the fourth and fifth statements, about the things you want and what you would change, are usually the ones worth sitting with.</p>
					<p>In a long illness the score can be low for entirely rational reasons: lost work, lost activities, an uncertain future. Some people nonetheless report satisfaction that surprises them, often after a period of adjustment, and the scale is sensitive enough to show that shift over months. A change of a few points is within normal fluctuation; a move across two bands is meaningful.</p>
					<p><strong>This is not a diagnosis.</strong> Low life satisfaction overlaps with, but is not the same as, depression. If you are struggling, tell your GP. If you ever feel unable to keep yourself safe, seek urgent help (in the UK: call 111 option 2, or 999 in an emergency).</p>
				</div>

				<button type="button" id="back-btn" class="back-btn">Edit answers</button>
			</div>
</Page>

<script>
const form = document.getElementById('swls-form') as HTMLFormElement;
const results = document.getElementById('results') as HTMLElement;

function getVal(i: number): number | null {
	const checked = document.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>(`input[name="q${i}"]:checked`);
	return checked ? parseInt(checked.value, 10) : null;
}

form.addEventListener('submit', (e) => {
	e.preventDefault();

	let total = 0;
	for (let i = 0; i < 5; i++) {
		const v = getVal(i);
		if (v === null) {
			const el = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>(`.question[data-index="${i}"]`);
			el?.classList.add('missing');
			el?.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'center' });
			setTimeout(() => el?.classList.remove('missing'), 2500);
			return;
		}
		total += v;
	}

	(document.getElementById('total-score-value') as HTMLElement).textContent = String(total);

	let band: string, note: string;
	if (total >= 31) { band = 'extremely satisfied'; note = 'Scores in this band are reported by people who feel their life is going very well in most respects. Whatever is supporting that is worth protecting, and it is a strong baseline to compare against later.'; }
	else if (total >= 26) { band = 'satisfied'; note = 'This is the most common band in general-population samples. Life is going well overall, even if some areas could be better, and small changes in circumstances tend to move scores within this range rather than out of it.'; }
	else if (total >= 21) { band = 'slightly satisfied'; note = 'Broadly positive with clear room for improvement. People here usually have specific areas of life they would like to change. In a long illness this is a common landing place once some adjustment has happened.'; }
	else if (total === 20) { band = 'neutral'; note = 'Equally satisfied and dissatisfied. Often this reflects a life in transition, or one where good and bad areas are balancing each other out.'; }
	else if (total >= 15) { band = 'slightly dissatisfied'; note = 'There are meaningful problems in some areas of life, though not across the board. It is worth asking which of the five statements you disagreed with most, because that is where the leverage is.'; }
	else if (total >= 10) { band = 'dissatisfied'; note = 'Substantial dissatisfaction, usually with several areas of life at once. In a chronic illness this is understandable and common, particularly early on or during a bad stretch. It is a reasonable thing to raise with your GP or a counsellor, and it can improve.'; }
	else { band = 'extremely dissatisfied'; note = 'Scores this low usually reflect life going badly in most areas at once. Please consider talking to your GP about how things are, and about what support might be available. Low satisfaction at this level often shifts with the right help.'; }

	(document.getElementById('score-interpretation') as HTMLElement).textContent =
		`Your SWLS score is ${total} out of 35, which falls in the "${band}" band. ${note}`;

	form.hidden = true;
	results.hidden = false;
	results.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' });
});

document.getElementById('reset-btn')?.addEventListener('click', () => form.reset());
document.getElementById('back-btn')?.addEventListener('click', () => {
	results.hidden = true;
	form.hidden = false;
	form.scrollIntoView({ behavior: 'smooth', block: 'start' });
});
</script>

<style>
	.tool-header {
		padding-bottom: 1.5em;
		border-bottom: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
		margin-bottom: 2.5em;
	}
	.tool-header h1 { margin: 0.3em 0 0.1em; }
	.subtitle { color: rgb(var(--gray)); font-size: 1.05em; margin: 0 0 0.75em; }
	.description { margin-bottom: 1em; }

	.form-instructions {
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
		font-style: italic;
		margin-bottom: 1.5em;
		font-size: 0.95em;
	}

	.question-grid { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0; }
	.question {
		padding: 1.1em 0;
		border-bottom: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
	}
	.question.missing { background: rgba(192, 57, 43, 0.05); border-radius: 6px; }
	.q-text { margin: 0 0 0.6em; font-size: 0.95em; }
	.q-num { font-weight: 700; margin-right: 0.25em; }
	.options { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: 0.4em; }
	.options label {
		display: flex;
		align-items: center;
		gap: 0.4em;
		cursor: pointer;
		font-size: 0.85em;
		padding: 0.35em 0.75em;
		border: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
		border-radius: 20px;
		transition: background 0.1s, border-color 0.1s;
		white-space: nowrap;
	}
	.options label:hover { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent); }
	.options label:has(input:checked) {
		background: var(--accent-light);
		border-color: var(--accent);
		color: var(--accent-dark);
		font-weight: 700;
	}
	.options input[type="radio"] { position: absolute; opacity: 0; width: 1px; height: 1px; margin: 0; }

	.form-footer { display: flex; gap: 1em; padding: 2em 0 1em; }
	button[type="submit"] {
		background: var(--accent); color: #fff; border: none;
		padding: 0.75em 2em; border-radius: 5px; font-size: 1em; font-weight: 700;
		cursor: pointer; font-family: inherit;
	}
	button[type="submit"]:hover { background: var(--accent-dark); }
	button[type="button"] {
		background: none; border: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
		padding: 0.75em 1.5em; border-radius: 5px; font-size: 0.95em;
		cursor: pointer; color: rgb(var(--gray)); font-family: inherit;
	}

	.results { padding-top: 1em; }
	.results h2 { margin-bottom: 0.5em; }
	.total-score { display: flex; align-items: baseline; gap: 0.5em; margin: 1em 0; }
	#total-score-value { font-size: 3.5em; font-weight: 700; color: var(--accent); line-height: 1; }
	.total-score-label { color: rgb(var(--gray)); font-size: 1.1em; }
	.score-interp {
		font-size: 1em; line-height: 1.6; margin: 0 0 2em;
		padding: 1em 1.25em; background: var(--accent-light);
		border-left: 4px solid var(--accent); border-radius: 4px;
	}
	.results-disclaimer {
		font-size: 0.88em;
		color: rgb(var(--gray-dark));
		border-top: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
		padding-top: 1.5em;
		line-height: 1.6;
	}
	.results-disclaimer h3 { font-size: 1em; color: rgb(var(--black)); margin-bottom: 0.5em; margin-top: 0; }
	.back-btn {
		background: none;
		border: 1px solid rgb(var(--gray-light));
		padding: 0.6em 1.25em;
		border-radius: 5px;
		font-size: 0.95em;
		cursor: pointer;
		color: rgb(var(--gray));
		font-family: inherit;
		margin-top: 1em;
	}
	.back-btn:hover { border-color: var(--accent); color: var(--accent); }

	@media (max-width: 600px) {
		.options { flex-direction: column; }
		.options label { white-space: normal; }
		.form-footer { flex-direction: column; }
	}
</style>
